NEW_NOTES = {
"D1": "Draw this on the board if you can: two strips, one long and one short. The long one is what travels on the network, 194 bytes; the short one leaves out marker, flag and witness and has 125 bytes. Ask which strip the txid is computed from. The short one; students expect the long one.",
"D2": "Point at the six cells one at a time: four for the version, one marker, one flag. Ask the room what an old parser does with the zero. It reads an empty input list and stops, which is exactly why the marker is a zero. A common mistake is to read the version as a big-endian number.",
"D3": "An input is only an address: the txid of an earlier transaction and an output number. Trace the arrow from Alice's input to output 1 of the earlier transaction. Ask where the amount is stored; in the earlier output, never in the input. Then point at the four sizes of CompactSize on the right.",
"D4": "This is the drawer comparison drawn as boxes. One coin goes in, two come out, and what is missing is the fee. Ask how many coins the drawer holds before and after. A common mistake is to look for the fee in the transaction; it is only a difference of two sums.",
"D5": "Show the 32 bits as one bar and point at three places: bit 31, bit 22 and the low 16 bits. Ask which meaning came first. Replacement, then the opt-in signal, then the relative timelock; the same four bytes were reused each time. Students forget that bit 31 switches the timelock off.",
"D6": "The bar is the 100,000 satoshis of the spent coin: 20,000 to Bob, 75,000 back as change and 5,000 left over for the miner. Below it, one output opened up: eight bytes of amount, a length and a lock. Ask who decides the fee. The sender, by leaving it out of the outputs.",
"D7": "Compare the two rows. Before segwit the signature sat inside the input script, so the txid covered it and a stranger could change it. After segwit the signature moved to the witness, outside the txid. Ask which brackets differ: the txid bracket shrinks, the wtxid bracket covers everything.",
"D8": "Two small pictures. The number line shows how one field is read as a height below 500 million and as a time above it. The timeline shows the 100-block wait of a coinbase coin. Ask what happens at block 108 and at block 109 for a coin made in block 9.",
"D9": "Same scale in all three bars. The 194 bytes become 569 weight units because every byte except marker, flag and witness counts four times. Dividing by four and rounding up gives 143 virtual bytes. Ask which bar a block limit is measured in: weight, four million per block.",
}
